    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-72716",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Suppose $X$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(X)=-13.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{2}$ (switching the second and the fifth rows). What is the value of $det(Y)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(-13) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
